    "title": "Boca Raton Works the Twelve-Month Clock",
    "epigraph": "A recovered stratum.",
    "body": [
      "Entry Systems Division decamped from New York for Florida and assembled a team of about a dozen core engineers and dozens of checker-players. Corporate review, normally glacial, was told to stand in the hallway.",
      "Off-the-shelf parts were the entire liturgy: standard chips, standard drives, standard plugs. The only truly custom silicon was the BIOS ROM, and nine months after kickoff a working prototype sat on a table doing something nobody could call premature."
    ],
    "doc": "Recovered from a payroll sheet note: Team of twelve core engineers plus supporting staff, led by Don Estridge. Site Boca Raton. Announcement target August 1981, one year from kickoff. Standard parts throughout—only BIOS falls under proprietary review.",
